1998 Sept 27: [rwp]

    I found that GetFITS.c was not checking the value of charsout after
    all write() operations to see if it returned -1, which means we had
    a fatal write error.  We found this when the folks at SAAO managed
    to fill an 8.5Gb data disk.  Surprisingly from the logs, Caliban
    went merrily along, reporting a blank rate (this gave an error
    message in Prospero whose status message parser gagged on the blank
    RATE=, which message caught their attention) as if nothing was
    wrong.  The clue was that we were getting log messages like:

	Sat Sep 26 21:12:09 1998 - Padded data with -1 bytes

    or

	Sat Sep 26 21:13:28 1998 - Wrote -1 header bytes

    the former was when the disk first hit the wall during a write, the
    latter during the write attempt for the subsequent image.  Further
    messages in this sequence were:

	Sat Sep 26 21:13:28 1998 - Looped 260 times
	Sat Sep 26 21:13:28 1998 - -260 data bytes written in 8192-byte chunks

    or getting -1 as a return from write() 260 times, which when summed gives
    -260.

    I made a pretty sloppy hack to GetFITS.c to check the value of
    charsout everytime there is a write() operation, and if it is -1, to
    check the value of errno returned.  If errno is ENOSPC, it means the
    disk is full.  If any other error, it tries to print ERRORSTR (no
    guarantees on all systems for that one...). It triggers a FATAL:
    error code, which should cause Prospero to abort the
    data-acquisition session and wait for further instructions.  At
    least in theory anyway.  We should do something nicer (and easier to
    modify) than just replicating the same block of code five times
    over.

1998 Sept 30: [brh]

    Added support for NOSWAP mode which takes effect when a fatal error
    occurs while trying to write to a full disk device.  At that point a
    flag in the systab is set (systab->noswap) which prevents further
    file transfers until the flag is cleared by issuing the +SWAP
    command to Caliban either at the console directly or across the
    serial port.  Updated the online help to detail the +SWAP command.
    Updated the online status display to indicate the swap mode.  Also
    updated the STATUS command to show the current swap mode.  Changed
    GetFITS so that it removes any files it was in the process of
    creating when the fatal disk full condition occurred.  Affected
    files: Caliban.c, Caliban.h, TransferDisk.c, AckDisk.c, CBStatus.c,
    GetFITS.c, and UseMount.c.

1998 Nov 3: [brh]

    Created the src/unicode directory which is a version of Caliban
    intended to be source code compatible across all 3 supported
    operating systems.  As of tonight it is Linux compatible.  It is
    also thought to be Solaris compatible but I am unable to test it
    since I can't log on as dts to the Solaris box.
    
    In order to come up with a source code compatible version, I had to
    abstract the I/O routines.  Whereas Solaris and SunOS use the
    standard read/write/lseek commands, Linux requires the use of the
    generic SCSI passthru interface (in order to achieve raw disk
    access) which does not support these commands.  To work around this
    problem, I created the CBread and CBseek routines which use a
    pre-processor #define (either LINUX or SOLARIS) to determine which
    routine to call, either read/lseek in the case of SOLARIS, or
    SGread/SGseek in the case of LINUX.

    The SGread/SGseek commands are based on the Linux generic SCSI
    interface which requires passing raw SCSI commands in through
    structures defined in sg.c and sg.h (in the
    /usr/src/linux/drivers/scsi directory).

    Also, at this point in time you must issue the following command to
    enable the advansys SCSI card:

        insmod advansys.o

